---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { BRAND } from "astro:schema";
---

<BaseLayout>
  <section>
    <div class="px-8 py-12 mx-auto md:px-12 lg:px-32 max-w-7xl lg:pb-24">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <h1
          class="text-5xl font-semibold text-center font-display text-blue-950 sm:text-6xl text-balance"
        >
          The four principles of the OnTrack Educational Framework
        </h1>

        <div class="mt-10 space-y-6 text-left text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
          <p>OTEP units can be designed in a variety of ways to suit the context, but they all adhere to the same 4 fundamental pedagogical principles.
<ol class="mt-3 space-y-3 list-disc list-outside pl-6">
              <li>
                
                 Active Learning: tasks designed to build and evidence learning are assessed as a whole portfolio of work.
               
                
              </li>

              <li>
                
                 Freedom to fail: Feedback cycles with no penalty for initial attempts allows for learning from mistakes which builds resilience and a growth mindset.
                
              </li>
                 
              <li>
                
                 Achieving competence: no marks are accumulated, with each task being assessed as satisfactory or not yet satisfactory, meaning students pass when they have demonstrated competence.
                
                
              </li>
              <li>
                
                  Guaranteeing standards: All pass level tasks in combination guarantee that passing students will meet all the unit learning outcomes.

                
                
              </li>

        
            </ol>

</p>
<h2 class="text-5xl font-semibold text-left font-display text-blue-750 sm:text-3xl text-balance">1. Active Learning</h2>
<p>
Combining learning tasks with assessment tasks ensures that students will focus on the learning. 

<strong>Why?</strong>
Because students are efficient. Assessment tasks are the focus of student effort and non-assessed learning tasks are generally ignored. 
<p>Examples that illustrate the point:</p>

<img src="/public/MATTERImages/examplesOfCombinedTasks.png" />

Learning resources/materials/opportunities are provided in whatever manner you prefer (textbooks, websites, video lessons, in person lectures, workshop activities, etc.) for students to refer to when attempting the tasks. MATTER does not prescribe how you must present your content.
</p>

<h2>2. Freedom to fail - learning from mistakes</h2>
<p>
Building in iterative formative feedback loops ensures that students can experiment, practice and improve their skills under guidance in a supportive low stakes environment
<strong>Why?</strong>
Because failing is learning. Teach students to not to fear mistakes, and to value advice. Enable them to build mastery by receiving and acting upon rich iterative feedback from an expert (you), and correcting their mistakes.
<p>Examples that illustrate the point:</p>
</p>
<p><img src="/public/MATTERImages/ExampleIterativeFeedbackDepth.png" /></p>

<p><img src="/public/MATTERImages/ExampleIterativeFeedbackBreadth.png" /></p>

<h2>3. Achieving competence</h2>
<p>
Remove the complexity from your unit design by removing marks. Instead of accumulating marks, students progress by demonstrating competency in undertaking a series of mastery based tasks.


<strong>Why?</strong>
Each task has a specific goal/focus and the only way to pass it is to demonstrate you’ve achieved that. Everything is a yes or no decision so there is no distraction from the purpose of the assessment. There’s no gaming of marks, no shortcuts the students can take, and no need to argue with a student over a given mark.
</p>
<p>Examples that illustrate the point:</p>

<p>coming soon...</p>

<h2>4. Guaranteeing standards</h2>
<p>
Designing a series of tasks that each focus on a different skill, knowledge or attitude enables you to explicitly guarantee that passing students have met the standards you have set for them on every metric. It also allows you to be very clear about the difference between grade levels and to design explicitly for both passing level students and higher grade students.

<strong>Why?</strong>
Because passing by marks accumulation does not guarantee that students have satisfactorily acquired all the necessary skills.  You wouldn’t give a student a degree if they got a HD in one unit and a fail in another. Why would you pass a student who didn’t demonstrate all the required skills and knowledge within a unit?
In passing a student you are warranting that they are competent to move forward in their education. </p>
